## 🛡️ Agent Guardrails – WayFinder Agent (Detailed)

These guardrails ensure the WayFinder Agent behaves responsibly, consistently, and safely, especially when handling sensitive or incomplete inputs.

| **Guardrail Type**             | **Purpose**                                                              | **How It’s Implemented in Waystar POC**                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **1. Scope Enforcement**       | Prevents the agent from responding to unsupported or off-topic requests. | - If a user asks something unrelated (e.g., “Set up payroll”), the agent replies:  \n→ *“I’m not configured to assist with that request. Please contact our support team.”*  \n- Handled via fallback prompt logic.      |
| **2. Response Boundaries**     | Avoids hallucinations or made-up responses. Only uses verified content.  | - All responses must come from:  \n  • Uploaded PDFs (e.g., DenialReasonCodes.pdf)  \n  • Approved Knowledge Articles (over 2k available)  \n- Responses are never generated from scratch.                               |
| **3. Intent Validation**       | Confirms that user inputs are complete and understandable before acting. | - For remittance checks:  \n→ *“Please provide a valid Payment ID to continue.”*  \n- For denial code explanations:  \n→ *“Can you confirm the denial code or claim number?”*                                            |
| **4. Role-Based Controls**     | Ensures different user experiences for internal vs. external users.      | - Internal users see options like *“Unlock user,” “Deactivate”*.  \n- External users see *“Reset password,” “Request access,” “Contact Support.”*  \n- Controlled via user permission sets and dynamic prompt branching. |
| **5. Data Sensitivity**        | Prevents agent from exposing or processing PHI/PII directly.             | - Agent avoids showing raw data like SSNs, emails, or patient names.  \n- If sensitive info is required, agent responds:  \n→ *“Please contact our live support team to complete this request securely.”*                |
| **6. Escalation Logic (HITL)** | Ensures agent knows when to escalate to a human agent.                   | - For unsupported, unclear, or failed flows, a case is automatically created via the `Create_Support_Case` flow.  \n- User is informed:  \n→ *“I’ve sent your request to a live agent. You’ll be contacted soon.”*       |
| **7. Missing Data Handling**   | Prevents broken or unclear flows when input is incomplete.               | - Agent uses fallback prompts like:  \n→ *“It looks like that Payment ID doesn't exist. Would you like to try again?”*  \n→ *“We couldn’t find records yet. Try again after an update.”*                                 |
| **8. Template-Based Output**   | Maintains consistent tone, structure, and clarity.                       | - All responses follow a set structure:  \n→ **Denial Summary**, **Remittance Result**, **Webinar List**, etc.  \n- Reduces ambiguity and improves UX.                                                                   |
| **9. Audit & Logging**         | Tracks user actions for security and troubleshooting.                    | - All user interactions and agent decisions are logged via Flow audit fields (e.g., Request, Result, Escalated\_Y/N).  \n- Helps validate performance during UAT and real usage.                                         |

